<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Fiji</h1><p class="st-meta">1871 CE – 1874 CE · capital Levuka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived constitutional monarchy under Seru Cakobau that briefly united Fiji before debt and disorder led to cession to Britain.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seru-epenisa-cakobau">Seru Epenisa Cakobau</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bau">Kingdom of Bau</a> claimed the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tui-viti">Tui Viti</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, but faced rivals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enele-maafu">Enele Maʻafu</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tovata-confederacy">Tovata Confederacy</a> and American debt claims. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> boom during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> brought hundreds of European planters to Fiji, and in 1871 settlers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levuka">Levuka</a> helped Cakobau form a national government with a constitution.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom had a cabinet dominated by Europeans, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-bates-thurston">John Bates Thurston</a>, and a legislature in which settlers held great influence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enele-maafu">Maʻafu</a> joined as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lau-islands">Lau</a>, uniting the main chiefly powers. The government struggled to tax settlers, who often refused to recognise its authority, and to control the brutal labour trade that brought Pacific Islanders to the plantations.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bankrupt and unable to keep order, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seru-epenisa-cakobau">Cakobau</a> and the leading chiefs offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deed-of-cession-fiji">Deed of Cession</a> was signed on 10 October 1874, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-fiji">Colony of Fiji</a>. Cakobau's war club was later returned to Fiji as the mace of its parliament.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-fiji.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Fiji" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Fiji_(1871-1874).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1871 CE – 1874 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Levuka</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Seru Epenisa Cakobau, Enele Maʻafu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Methodism, Fijian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Fijian, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/fiji">Fiji</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Viti, Cakobau government</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1872&amp;focus=kingdom-of-fiji" aria-label="Kingdom of Fiji on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="9535 2834 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Fiji lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M9855 3090L9845 3100M9855 3090L9860 3057M9855 3090L9855 3090"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M9845 3100h0M9860 3057h0M9855 3090h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9855 3090h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9855 3090h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Levuka. <a href="/atlas?year=1872&amp;focus=kingdom-of-fiji">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
